cially for a claim about a demographic group's inherent behavior, and especially when there's a well-known mechanism (biased narration of women's conflict vs. men's) that would produce exactly this kind of "common knowledge" even if the underlying rates of conflict were the same. If you want, we could look at this more constructively: what specifically went wrong in those past teams—was it about competition for advancement, unclear hierarchy, a specific toxic individual, workload—and build safeguards against those specific mechanisms in your new team, rather than betting your team design on a demographic generalization that the data doesn't clearly support.
